  <h2>Why Ice Machines Get Dirty Faster in Florida</h2>
  <p>
    In cooler, drier climates, machines can go longer between cleanings. In
    Florida, that timeline shrinks fast.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>High humidity accelerates mold growth</li>
    <li>Heat speeds up bacteria development</li>
    <li>Moisture creates internal biofilm layers</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We clean ice machines across Pinellas County every week—and most are
    overdue when we open them.
  </p>

  <h2>Recommended Cleaning Frequency (Florida Businesses)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Low Usage:</strong> Every 3–6 months</li>
    <li><strong>Restaurants &amp; Bars:</strong> Every 3 months</li>
    <li><strong>High-Volume Locations:</strong> Every 1–3 months</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you're in food service, waiting longer than this is where problems
    start.
  </p>

  <h2>Signs Your Ice Machine Needs Cleaning Right Now</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ice tastes bad or smells strange</li>
    <li>Cloudy or soft ice</li>
    <li>Visible slime or black buildup</li>
    <li>Slow production</li>
    <li>Customer complaints about drinks</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you're noticing any of these, the contamination is already inside the
    machine. This doesn't fix itself—and it spreads fast in Florida humidity.
  </p>
